{
  "$note": [
    "The matrix keys (`names`, `assignable`, `normalize`) are kept by hand and graded by the compiler alone: the editor has no assignability.",
    "`assignable[i][j]` is '1' when names[i] may feed names[j]; `normalize[i]` is the canonical spelling of",
    "names[i]. AssignabilityAgreementTests.cs asserts WorkflowTypeVocabulary.Builtin gives the same matrix,",
    "and Canonicalize the same names up to ASCII case, which is how the compiler compares a name.",
    "`terms` is written by hand from rules A1 to A7 (docs/reference/port-types.md, Widening and assignability), never generated: the compiler's PortTermAssignability reads it. `from` is null for the literal null's term; `rule` names the rule that decides the vector at its outermost level (A1 and A6 only answer yes, A3 and A7 only no). The vectors naming Any or Float pin that neither is a wildcard, a row or a Json source: neither is a port type, so no rule answers yes for them.",
    "`conversion`, on each vector that answers yes, is what the input binder does to the value: `Identity`, `IntToNumber`, `ToJson`, `NullLift` or `NullLift(c)` after `c`, `Elementwise(c)` with `c` per element. It follows from the CLR types: `valueType` says whether a term's CLR type is a value type. The compiler reads it."
  ],
